  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n===Dry Today with Clouds over the Bluegrass===\n\nCurrently, there are calm to light winds and clear skies over the \nregion. This has allowed for efficient radiational cooling, bringing \ntemperatures into the upper 20s and low 30s. Patchy fog is possible \nthis morning, and given freezing temperatures, there may be a few \nslick spots on elevated roads.\n\nAs deep troughing slowly pushes to the northeast, a band of \nvorticity is moving south and into the northeastern portions of the \nregion. This feature, coupled with northerly fetch from Lake \nMichigan, will bring broken to overcast low level clouds. These \nclouds will push northeast of the region this evening and into the \novernight hours. Given cloud coverage over the Bluegrass today, \nhighs will remain a bit lower in the upper 40s and low 50s. The rest \nof the region will see highs in the mid 50s. Overnight, calm winds \nand clear skies will allow temperatures to cool into the mid-upper \n20s for most and low 30s in urban areas. Patchy fog will be possible \nearly Monday morning. \n\n===Dry and Warming Each Day This Week===\n\nRidging over the central Plains will gradually push east and amplify \nthrough mid week. At the surface, strong high pressure will be \ncentered right over the CWA early this week and reach near 1036mb. \nThis setup will allow for temperatures to warm 3-5 degrees each day \nthrough Wednesday, reaching the mid-upper 60s. Calm and clear nights \nwill allow for efficient radiational cooling, therefore, lows will \nbe in the 20s and 30s. \n\nThursday, troughing will move through the Great Lakes region and \nforce upper ridging and surface high pressure to push to the east. \nWinds will become southerly, which will allow temperatures to \ncontinue to warm into the upper 60s and possibly the low 70s by \nSaturday. Overnight lows will be in the upper 30s and low 40s. Dry \nconditions will persist through the end of the week. \n\nOver the weekend, there is a fair amount of agreement amongst \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance to bring a low pressure system \nthrough the Great Lakes region. This could be our next chance for a \ncold front to bring rainfall.",
